## Tool Class — `WeatherTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetCurrentWeather(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'London' or 'New York'")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching weather for {City}", city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/v1/current.json?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a multi-day weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        [Description("Number of forecast days (1-7)")] int days,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching {Days}-day forecast for {City}", days, city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/v1/forecast.json?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&days={days}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

## Program.cs — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient<WeatherTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weatherapi.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

### 1. Constructor injection (non-static class) — shown above
The class is instantiated per-request by the DI container. Services like `HttpClient` and `ILogger<T>` are injected via the primary constructor. Register services normally in `Program.cs`.

### 2. Method parameter injection (static class)
DI services appear directly as method parameters — the SDK resolves them automatically and **excludes them from the tool's JSON schema**:

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public static class StaticTools
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Ping a URL.")]
    public static async Task<string> Ping(
        [Description("URL to check")] string url,
        HttpClient httpClient,               // ← injected, hidden from LLM
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)  // ← also injected
    {
        var r = await httpClient.GetAsync(url, cancellationToken);
        return $"Status: {(int)r.StatusCode}";
    }
}
```

**Auto-injected types** (never appear in the tool schema):
| Type | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `CancellationToken` | Cooperative cancellation |
| `IMcpServer` | Server instance (notifications, logging) |
| `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>` | Report progress to client |
| Any DI-registered service | `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, custom services |

Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM. Everything else is injected silently by the framework.
